    "result_text": "All 22 tests pass. The implementation is complete and green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `namesakeCount` feature for `POST /api/owners` in this Spring PetClinic REST app, following the existing pattern used by computed fields like `customerCode` and `householdId`.\n\n**What `namesakeCount` returns:** the number of existing owners (before this create) sharing the same `firstName` and `lastName`, compared case-insensitively. The owner being created is not counted, since the value is computed before it is persisted.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `namesakeCount` integer property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it in GET responses.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added a persisted `namesakeCount` field (`@Column(name = \"namesake_count\")`) with getter/setter, so the snapshot value survives and is returned by `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\n3. **DB schemas** (`db/hsqldb`, `db/h2`, `db/mysql`, `db/postgres`) \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column to the `owners` table (tests use the HSQLDB schema with `ddl-auto=none`).\n\n4. **`.../rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 in `addOwner`, compute the count via a new `countNamesakes(firstName, lastName)` helper (case-insensitive match over existing owners) and set it on the owner just before saving. MapStruct maps the field into the DTO automatically by name.\n\n**Verification:** `Cp13Tests` (the new namesake test) passes, and the full suite is green \u2014 **22 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.",
